// Showing what a sequential container holds, without disturbing it.
//
// A container cannot implement `Debug` itself: what a `Vector<T>` looks like is entirely a question about `T`, and
// an interface implementation has nowhere to say "when the element can be shown". So these are handwritten
// functions carrying the bound — `WriteVectorDebug<T: Debug>` — which is the shape the language does have. Each
// instantiation resolves its element's `WriteDebug` at compile time; nothing is dispatched at run time.
//
// They borrow, and that is the whole design. A container owns its elements and hands out no copies of a move-only
// one, so a diagnostic that took an element out to print it would be taking ownership of it — which is exactly what
// a caller with a container they still need cannot afford. Every element here is shown through a borrow of it, so a
// vector of a move-only type can be written as many times as wanted and still holds everything afterwards.
//
// A vector and an array are one run of elements, so both hand `Rux/Text` their slice and it does the rest. A deque
// is the exception, and the reason is its shape rather than an oversight: its elements live in a ring, so a deque
// that has wrapped holds them in two runs with a gap between. It writes them as one sequence — which is what they
// are to a reader — by walking both runs inside one pair of brackets, which is the one thing the slice writer
// cannot be asked to do.
//
// Maps and sets are here too, written `{key: value, key: value}` and `{element, element}` — braces rather than
// brackets, because what a map holds is not a run of things and reading one as a sequence would be reading it
// wrongly. The two kinds differ in exactly one respect, and it is the only interesting question they raise.
//
// An ordered map or set is written in key order, which is the order it keeps and the order every walk of it takes,
// so its rendering is a fact about its contents and nothing else. A hash map or set is written in the order of its
// table, which is a fact about the container rather than about what it holds: it follows from the seed drawn when
// the container was made and from every insertion and removal since, because displacement decides which of two
// entries that want the same slot gets it. So two containers holding exactly the same entries render differently,
// and so does the same container in the next run of the same program. That is documented rather than fixed: sorting
// would need an ordering on the key that a hash container does not have and was built not to need, and inventing
// one would make every hash container require something extra just so that its diagnostic looked tidy. A caller who
// needs a stable order has an ordered map.
//
// `CollectionError` presents itself here as well, for the same reason the containers do — this package depends on
// `Rux/Text` and Text does not depend on it, so this is the side of the edge the implementation can live on.
// `Display` is the description a reader is shown and `Debug` names the case, exactly as for the errors Text
// presents. The descriptions are stable and are not a parsing contract: a program branches on the case.

import Core::{ Result, Unit };
import Text::{ Debug, Display, FormatError, FormatSpec, RejectStyle, TextWriter, WriteAscii, WriteBytes,
               WriteSliceDebug };

// One run of elements written into an open sequence, with `written` saying how many are already there so that the
// separator lands between elements and never before the first.
func WriteRun<T: Debug>(writer: &var TextWriter, items: T[..], spec: FormatSpec,
                        written: uint) -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
    var index: uint = 0;
    while index < items.length {
        if written + index != 0 {
            WriteBytes(writer, ", ")?;
        }
        items.data[index].WriteDebug(writer, spec)?;
        index = index + 1;
    }
    return Result::Success<Unit, FormatError>(Unit {});
}

/// Writes a borrowed vector as `[first, second, third]`.
///
/// An empty vector is `[]`, and its capacity is not shown: the spare room past the length holds nothing, so it is
/// not part of what the vector is. The vector and every element are borrowed, so this may be called on a vector of
/// a move-only element type and the vector still holds all of them afterwards.
///
/// `spec` is handed to each element unchanged and means whatever that element type makes of it. The brackets and
/// commas are punctuation and are never padded.
///
/// # Failures
/// Reports whatever the writer or an element's implementation reported, stopping at the first refusal.
///
/// @typeParam T the element type, which must know how to show itself
/// @param writer the destination
/// @param value the vector to show, borrowed for the call
/// @param spec what to show each element under
/// @returns success when the whole rendering was accepted
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/collections/write-vector-debug
pub func WriteVectorDebug<T: Debug>(writer: &var TextWriter, value: &Vector<T>,
                                    spec: FormatSpec) -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
    return WriteSliceDebug<T>(writer, value.AsSlice(), spec);
}

/// Writes a borrowed array as `[first, second, third]`.
///
/// An empty array is `[]`. The array and every element are borrowed, so an array of a move-only element type is
/// shown without disturbing one of them.
///
/// `spec` describes the elements, exactly as for a vector.
///
/// # Failures
/// Reports whatever the writer or an element's implementation reported, stopping at the first refusal.
///
/// @typeParam T the element type, which must know how to show itself
/// @param writer the destination
/// @param value the array to show, borrowed for the call
/// @param spec what to show each element under
/// @returns success when the whole rendering was accepted
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/collections/write-array-debug
pub func WriteArrayDebug<T: Debug>(writer: &var TextWriter, value: &Array<T>,
                                   spec: FormatSpec) -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
    return WriteSliceDebug<T>(writer, value.AsSlice(), spec);
}

/// Writes a borrowed deque as `[front, ..., back]`, front element first.
///
/// The order is the deque's own — what `PopFront` would yield, in the order it would yield it — whether or not the
/// ring has wrapped. A wrapped deque holds its elements in two runs, and both are walked inside one pair of
/// brackets, so where the ring happens to be split is not visible in the output and does not need to be.
///
/// Nothing is rearranged to write it: this is the reason the deque is borrowed rather than borrowed mutably, since
/// making the elements contiguous first would be a diagnostic quietly moving a caller's data around.
///
/// `spec` describes the elements, exactly as for a vector.
///
/// # Failures
/// Reports whatever the writer or an element's implementation reported, stopping at the first refusal.
///
/// @typeParam T the element type, which must know how to show itself
/// @param writer the destination
/// @param value the deque to show, borrowed for the call
/// @param spec what to show each element under
/// @returns success when the whole rendering was accepted
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/collections/write-deque-debug
pub func WriteDequeDebug<T: Debug>(writer: &var TextWriter, value: &Deque<T>,
                                   spec: FormatSpec) -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
    var front: T[..];
    var back: T[..];
    value.AsSlices(@front, @back);

    WriteAscii(writer, c8'[')?;
    WriteRun<T>(writer, front, spec, 0)?;
    WriteRun<T>(writer, back, spec, front.length)?;
    return WriteAscii(writer, c8']');
}

/// Writes a borrowed hash map as `{key: value, key: value}`.
///
/// An empty map is `{}`. Braces rather than brackets, because a map is not a run of elements and reading its
/// rendering as one would be reading it wrongly.
///
/// The order is the table's, which follows from the seed the map was made with and from every insertion and removal
/// since — so it is not the insertion order, it does not hold between two maps with the same entries, and it changes
/// between runs of the same program. Nothing here sorts, since that would need an ordering on the key that a hash
/// map does not have. `TreeMap` is the container whose rendering is stable.
///
/// Every key and value is shown through a borrow of it, so a map of move-only entries is shown without taking one
/// out. `spec` is handed to each of them unchanged.
///
/// # Failures
/// Reports whatever the writer or an entry's implementation reported, stopping at the first refusal.
///
/// @typeParam K the key type, which must know how to show itself
/// @typeParam V the value type, which must know how to show itself
/// @param writer the destination
/// @param value the map to show, borrowed for the call
/// @param spec what to show each key and value under
/// @returns success when the whole rendering was accepted
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/collections/write-hash-map-debug
pub func WriteHashMapDebug<K: Debug, V: Debug>(writer: &var TextWriter, value: &HashMap<K, V>,
                                               spec: FormatSpec) -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
    WriteAscii(writer, c8'{')?;
    var index: uint = 0;
    var written: uint = 0;
    while index < value.table.Capacity() {
        if value.table.IsOccupied(index) {
            if written != 0 {
                WriteBytes(writer, ", ")?;
            }
            value.table.keys[index].WriteDebug(writer, spec)?;
            WriteBytes(writer, ": ")?;
            value.table.values[index].WriteDebug(writer, spec)?;
            written = written + 1;
        }
        index = index + 1;
    }
    return WriteAscii(writer, c8'}');
}

/// Writes a borrowed hash set as `{first, second, third}`.
///
/// An empty set is `{}`. The order is the table's and carries the same warning as a hash map's: it follows from the
/// seed and from what the set has been through, so it is not the insertion order and does not hold between runs.
/// `TreeSet` is the ordered one.
///
/// Every element is shown through a borrow of it, and `spec` is handed to each unchanged.
///
/// # Failures
/// Reports whatever the writer or an element's implementation reported, stopping at the first refusal.
///
/// @typeParam T the element type, which must know how to show itself
/// @param writer the destination
/// @param value the set to show, borrowed for the call
/// @param spec what to show each element under
/// @returns success when the whole rendering was accepted
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/collections/write-hash-set-debug
pub func WriteHashSetDebug<T: Debug>(writer: &var TextWriter, value: &HashSet<T>,
                                     spec: FormatSpec) -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
    WriteAscii(writer, c8'{')?;
    var index: uint = 0;
    var written: uint = 0;
    while index < value.table.Capacity() {
        if value.table.IsOccupied(index) {
            if written != 0 {
                WriteBytes(writer, ", ")?;
            }
            value.table.keys[index].WriteDebug(writer, spec)?;
            written = written + 1;
        }
        index = index + 1;
    }
    return WriteAscii(writer, c8'}');
}

/// Writes a borrowed ordered map as `{key: value, key: value}`, in key order.
///
/// An empty map is `{}`. The order is the map's own — the order `Iterate` walks, the order `First` and `Last` name
/// the ends of — so two maps holding the same entries render identically and one map renders identically every
/// time. That is what makes an ordered map's rendering usable in an assertion where a hash map's is not.
///
/// The walk crosses every edge of the tree twice and allocates nothing, and nothing is taken out of the map: every
/// key and value is shown through a borrow of the node holding it. `spec` is handed to each unchanged.
///
/// # Failures
/// Reports whatever the writer or an entry's implementation reported, stopping at the first refusal.
///
/// @typeParam K the key type, which must know how to show itself
/// @typeParam V the value type, which must know how to show itself
/// @param writer the destination
/// @param value the map to show, borrowed for the call
/// @param spec what to show each key and value under
/// @returns success when the whole rendering was accepted
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/collections/write-tree-map-debug
pub func WriteTreeMapDebug<K: Debug, V: Debug>(writer: &var TextWriter, value: &TreeMap<K, V>,
                                               spec: FormatSpec) -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
    WriteAscii(writer, c8'{')?;
    var node = Tree::Minimum<K, V>(value.tree.root);
    var written: uint = 0;
    while node != null {
        if written != 0 {
            WriteBytes(writer, ", ")?;
        }
        node.key.WriteDebug(writer, spec)?;
        WriteBytes(writer, ": ")?;
        node.value.WriteDebug(writer, spec)?;
        written = written + 1;
        node = Tree::Successor<K, V>(node);
    }
    return WriteAscii(writer, c8'}');
}

/// Writes a borrowed ordered set as `{first, second, third}`, in order.
///
/// An empty set is `{}`. The order is the set's own, so the rendering is stable between sets holding the same
/// elements and between runs, unlike a hash set's.
///
/// Every element is shown through a borrow of the node holding it, and `spec` is handed to each unchanged.
///
/// # Failures
/// Reports whatever the writer or an element's implementation reported, stopping at the first refusal.
///
/// @typeParam T the element type, which must know how to show itself
/// @param writer the destination
/// @param value the set to show, borrowed for the call
/// @param spec what to show each element under
/// @returns success when the whole rendering was accepted
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/collections/write-tree-set-debug
pub func WriteTreeSetDebug<T: Debug>(writer: &var TextWriter, value: &TreeSet<T>,
                                     spec: FormatSpec) -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
    WriteAscii(writer, c8'{')?;
    var node = Tree::Minimum<T, Unit>(value.tree.root);
    var written: uint = 0;
    while node != null {
        if written != 0 {
            WriteBytes(writer, ", ")?;
        }
        node.key.WriteDebug(writer, spec)?;
        written = written + 1;
        node = Tree::Successor<T, Unit>(node);
    }
    return WriteAscii(writer, c8'}');
}

// Whether the specification is one an error description can answer. Neither a style nor a precision is: a
// description has one form, and truncating it produces a sentence that says something else.
func AcceptsErrorSpec(spec: FormatSpec) -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
    RejectStyle(spec)?;
    if spec.HasPrecision() { return Result::Error<Unit, FormatError>(FormatError::UnsupportedRequest); }
    return Result::Success<Unit, FormatError>(Unit {});
}

extend CollectionError : Display {
    /// Writes the description of why an operation did not happen, or `no error` for success.
    ///
    /// The five cases stay distinguishable in words as they are in the type: a shortage of memory is not a size that
    /// cannot be named, and neither is a layout the allocator refuses outright. Those distinctions decide whether a
    /// caller retries, so a description that blurred them would send somebody to fix the wrong thing.
    ///
    /// The descriptions are stable. They are not a parsing contract: a program branches on the case.
    ///
    /// # Failures
    /// Reports whatever the writer reported, and `UnsupportedRequest` for any style or precision.
    ///
    /// @param writer the destination
    /// @param spec what the placeholder asked for
    /// @returns success when the whole description was accepted
    /// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/collections/collection-error/write-display
    pub func WriteDisplay(self: &CollectionError, writer: &var TextWriter,
                          spec: FormatSpec) -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
        AcceptsErrorSpec(spec)?;
        return match self {
            .None => WriteBytes(writer, "no error"),
            .OutOfMemory => WriteBytes(writer, "out of memory"),
            .CapacityOverflow => WriteBytes(writer, "size that cannot be represented"),
            .IndexOutOfRange => WriteBytes(writer, "index the collection does not have"),
            .Unsupported => WriteBytes(writer, "layout this allocator cannot serve")
        };
    }
}

extend CollectionError : Debug {
    /// Names the case, qualified by its type: `CollectionError::OutOfMemory`.
    ///
    /// # Failures
    /// Reports whatever the writer reported, and `UnsupportedRequest` for any style or precision.
    ///
    /// @param writer the destination
    /// @param spec what the placeholder asked for
    /// @returns success when the whole name was accepted
    /// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/collections/collection-error/write-debug
    pub func WriteDebug(self: &CollectionError, writer: &var TextWriter,
                        spec: FormatSpec) -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
        AcceptsErrorSpec(spec)?;
        WriteBytes(writer, "CollectionError::")?;
        return match self {
            .None => WriteBytes(writer, "None"),
            .OutOfMemory => WriteBytes(writer, "OutOfMemory"),
            .CapacityOverflow => WriteBytes(writer, "CapacityOverflow"),
            .IndexOutOfRange => WriteBytes(writer, "IndexOutOfRange"),
            .Unsupported => WriteBytes(writer, "Unsupported")
        };
    }
}
